{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1749"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_926498109#10_1925545491","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1749","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_926498109#10_1925545491\" , \"end_char\": 5348.000000 , \"headings\": \"How AT&T Works How AT&T Works The Beginning Theodore Vail One Policy, One System: Universal Service What AT&T Did (and Still Does) Transmission Switching Management The End of Monopoly After the Breakup SBC: Ma Bell's Heir Apparent? \" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Western Union had seen possibilities in the \"electrical toy, \"after all. They had just started offering their own telephone </cover>service using Bell telephones. Vail began legal proceedings against Western Union for patent infringement. Western Union was a huge company -- it had money to spend and an army of patent lawyers. Against huge odds, Vail enlisted a legal army of his own. With the law on his side, and an uncompromising attitude, Vail forced a favorable settlement with Western Union. The news of the deal was explosive, and overnight Bell Telephone leapt into the communications arena as a preeminent power. Advertisement After the settlement, Bell Telephone's fortunes and stock price rose steadily. In 1881, Bell issued its first annual report, which "}}
