{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_313563554#5_657172409","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage discusses Bell's telephone breakthrough and says it caught people off-guard, but it does not directly address whether people trusted, distrusted, feared, or accepted the telephone when it was invented.","learned":"Some histories frame Bell's telephone as surprising and more important than expected, but not necessarily as mistrusted."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ telephone telephones) (+ \"afraid to use\" feared scared fearful suspicious) (+ early first introduced invented))","reason":"Search for direct evidence of fear or suspicion toward using the telephone when it was first introduced, another likely form of lack of trust."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ telephone telephones) (+ \"afraid to use\" feared scared fearful suspicious) (+ early first introduced invented))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2078195365#9_3134542286","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1756","rank":1,"snippet":"Tobacco,” calling it a disgusting habit of godless savages and worse—of Frenchmen and Spaniards. Smoking was considered a deadly sin in Russia and the Middle East, and Pope Clement VIII threatened anyone smoking in a holy place with excommunication. 6 The Telephone Writer Ambrose Bierce once defined the telephone as “an invention of the devil which abrogates some of the advantages of making a disagreeable person keep his distance.” He was being satirical, but when the telephone was invented, there were many who viewed it with suspicion and suspected a diabolical origin. When the <cover>telephone was first introduced in Sweden, many rural folks feared </cover>it was magical and that telephone wires would attract lightning or evil spirits. Preachers outright decried the telephone as an instrument of the Devil, and lines were often pulled down or sabotaged by irate farmers. The Amish and Old Order Mennonites of Pennsylvania had a similar view.\" , \"start_char\": 6017.000000 , \"title\": \"10 Common Things People Believed Were Invented By The Devil - Listverse\" , \"url\": \"http://listverse.com/2016/01/23/10-common-things-people-believed-were-invented-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"19/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2078195365#9_3134542286","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly says many people viewed the newly invented telephone with suspicion, feared it was magical, thought it had diabolical origins, and even sabotaged lines. However, the document is a general listicle about things believed invented by the devil rather than a document dedicated to early telephone trust, so it is not a top-grade source.","learned":"Some early users, especially rural people in Sweden and some religious communities, distrusted the telephone, associating it with magic, lightning, evil spirits, or the devil."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ telephone (+ devil diabolical magical \"evil spirits\" lightning) (+ sweden rural farmers suspicion))","reason":"Follow the strong evidence from the latest result with a focused query for dedicated historical sources about early fear and suspicion of the telephone as magical, dangerous, or diabolical."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ telephone (+ devil diabolical magical \"evil spirits\" lightning) (+ sweden rural farmers suspicion))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2078195365#10_3134544323","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1757","rank":1,"snippet":"People Believed Were Invented By The Devil 10 Common Things People Believed Were Invented By The Devil 10 Forks 9 Musical Instruments 8 Socialism 7 Smoking 6 The Telephone 5 Dice 4 The Smurfs 3 The Cross 2 Soccer 1 Theater \" , \"segment\": \"6 The Telephone Writer Ambrose Bierce once defined the telephone as “an invention of the devil which abrogates some of the advantages of making a disagreeable person keep his distance.” He was being satirical, but when the telephone was invented, there were many who viewed it with <cover>suspicion and suspected a diabolical origin. When the telephone </cover>was first introduced in Sweden, many rural folks feared it was magical and that telephone wires would attract lightning or evil spirits. Preachers outright decried the telephone as an instrument of the Devil, and lines were often pulled down or sabotaged by irate farmers. The Amish and Old Order Mennonites of Pennsylvania had a similar view. Their societies were informed by gelassenheit, a sense of belonging and separation from the outside world driven by dress, language, tradition, nonverbal communication, and a moral code. New forms of communication were perceived as a threat to face-to-face "}}
