{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_713730453#1_1345227072","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly supports the information need: in 1876 a Western Union internal memo said Bell's telephone had too many shortcomings to be seriously considered and was of no value to them, showing early lack of trust in the invention as a means of communication.","learned":"Western Union reportedly rejected the 1876 telephone as having too many shortcomings and being of no value, despite its later rapid growth."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1769","reason":"This query produced highly direct evidence via the Western Union rejection quote; continue to find additional documents with the same or similar direct evidence of early skepticism."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1769"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1339911292#13_2782357850","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1769","rank":2,"snippet":"Vision Motivating your team to believe it can be done Communication Management Path Tight Scope Statement Risk Management Change Mangement Quality Mangement Stress Management Lesson Learned In Conclusion \" , \"segment\": \"For the purposes of this paper, a brief highlight of the topic will be given with further elaboration to be given with examples a the conference presentation. Vision Behind every good project, a great leader should be found. Lack of leadership vision can have detrimental effects on a project and opportunities could be lost. Consider this statement from the <cover>Western Union Internal Memo 1876, “ The ‘telephone’ has too many shortcomings </cover>to be seriously considered as a means of communication. The device is inherently of no value to us” (Western Union Internal memo 1876, 2005). The ability to be an innovator and see beyond the challenges and focus on the goal is an attribute that makes a successful Project Manager. Motivating your team to believe it can be done Shorten interval projects are riddled with risks and can be difficult to work on. For that matter, the project manager has an important task to motivate the project team. John Woods eloquently pointed out that “You don’"}}
