{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_651027006#12_625728673","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage is about Bell offering telephone patent rights to Western Union, early private lines and switchboards, and Bell's own lack of business interest. It does not directly say whether people trusted or distrusted the telephone when invented.","learned":"An Engineering and Technology History Wiki article may contain useful early telephone business context, including Western Union turning down Bell's offer, but the visible passage does not directly address trust."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_651027006#12_625728673","reason":"The source appears to be a substantial telephone/Bell history from an engineering history wiki; the full document may contain more direct evidence about Western Union's rejection or early public skepticism than the snippet shows."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_651027006#12_625728673"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_651027006#12_625728673","document":"Alexander Graham Bell - Engineering and Technology History Wiki ... Alexander Graham Bell Alexander Graham Bell Alexander Graham Bell President of AIEE Biography Images Further Reading  ... Trying to make some quick cash, Bell offered Western Union the patent rights to the telephone for $100,000. The telegraph company had a nationwide network of wires in place and could naturally have branched out. Content with its telegraph monopoly, Western Union turned down Bell’s offer and lost the chance to monopolize another lucrative industry. By the summer of 1877, the telephone had become a business. The first private lines, which typically connected a businessman’s home and his office, had been placed in service. The first commercial telephone switchboard opened the following year in New Haven. Bell had little interest in being a businessman. In July 1877, he married Mabel Hubbard, and set out for what proved a long honeymoon in England. He left the growing Bell Telephone Company to Hubbard and Sanders, and went on to a long and productive career as an independent researcher and inventor. In 1880, he invented and patented the photophone, which transmitted voices over beams of light.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
