{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_258086360#17_466991078","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage is in an Alexander Graham Bell/telephone context and directly says Western Union's leaders believed the telephone was just a fad and would not be profitable, then later recognized its value. This is direct evidence that important contemporaries did not initially trust the invention's usefulness.","learned":"Another early skepticism pattern was that Western Union thought the telephone was a fad and not profitable before reversing its view by 1878."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1789","reason":"This query produced strong direct evidence about early skepticism toward the telephone as a fad/novelty; with limited budget remaining, continue this productive result list for one more potentially grade-3 document."}}
